{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
<h1>{{ page.title }}</h1>

<h2 id="numbers">What the numbers mean</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Accepted abstracts only.</strong> The booklets list abstracts that were accepted, and authors can ask for
a poster. “Talk rate” is the fraction of accepted abstracts presented as a talk (platform or plenary); it is not an
acceptance rate or a quality score.</li>
<li><strong>Associations, not causes.</strong> A phrase that is common in talks may simply mark a field or a large
consortium that tends to get talks; it does not make an abstract more likely to be picked.</li>
<li><strong>Small numbers are noisy.</strong> Every share comes with a 95% interval; wide intervals mean little can be
said.</li>
<li><strong>What is not shown.</strong> The site never names an institution, country, metro area or person for having
no talks: those rows are left out of the tables and figures here. World regions are shown in full. The complete
aggregate tables are in the <a href="{{ repo_url }}/tree/main/outputs">repository</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Not every abstract is matched.</strong> Institution, metro and country tables only count abstracts whose
affiliations could be matched; see below.</li>
</ul>

{% macro pct(v) %}{{ "%.0f"|format(100 * v) }}%{% endmacro %}
<h2 id="match-rates">Match rates</h2>
<p>The share of abstracts whose author could be placed. An abstract counts as matched if at least one of the author's
affiliations resolves to one of the {{ n_institutes }} listed institutions (or a metro area, or a country). Unmatched
abstracts still count toward the totals, the talk rate and the phrases, but drop out of the institution, metro and
country tables. The institution list was built from the institutions with the most abstracts, so unmatched abstracts
come mostly from institutions with fewer: rankings describe the listed institutions, not everyone.{% if years[-1].summary.first_inst_coverage < 0.68 %} In {{ years[-1].year }} fewer affiliations
matched ({{ pct(years[-1].summary.first_inst_coverage) }} of first authors), so its institution and metro
tables cover fewer abstracts.{% endif %} Senior-author rates are over abstracts not cut off in the booklet.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="match">
<thead><tr><th class="text" rowspan="2">Year</th><th rowspan="2">Abstracts</th><th colspan="3" class="group">First author</th>
<th colspan="3" class="group">Senior author</th></tr>
<tr><th>Institution</th><th>Metro</th><th>Country</th><th>Institution</th><th>Metro</th><th>Country</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for y in years %}{% set s = y.summary %}<tr><td class="text">{{ y.year }}</td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(s.abstracts) }}</td>
<td>{{ pct(s.first_inst_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.first_metro_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.first_country_coverage) }}</td>
<td>{{ pct(s.senior_inst_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.senior_metro_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.senior_country_coverage) }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
{% if pooled %}{% set s = pooled.summary %}<tr class="total"><td class="text">All years</td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(s.abstracts) }}</td>
<td>{{ pct(s.first_inst_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.first_metro_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.first_country_coverage) }}</td>
<td>{{ pct(s.senior_inst_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.senior_metro_coverage) }}</td><td>{{ pct(s.senior_country_coverage) }}</td></tr>{% endif %}
</tbody></table></div>

{% if "trends_talk_rate" in trends %}
<h2 id="talk-rate">Talk rate by year</h2>
<p>The share of accepted abstracts presented as a talk ranges from
{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * (years | map(attribute="summary.talk_rate") | min)) }}% to
{{ "%.1f"|format(100 * (years | map(attribute="summary.talk_rate") | max)) }}% by year. Every talk rate on this site is
compared with its own year's rate, or with the pooled rate for all years.</p>
<figure class="fig"><a href="img/trends_talk_rate.png"><img src="img/trends_talk_rate.png" alt="Talk rate per year: the share of accepted abstracts presented as a talk, with each year's talk and abstract counts and host city." loading="lazy"></a></figure>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="built">How it's built</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Parsing.</strong> Text is extracted with PyMuPDF and split into abstracts using each booklet's layout
(session headers, titles, author blocks, “Abstract:” labels). Every parsed abstract is checked against the booklet's
table of contents, and a hand-checked sample is used as a test set. Abstracts cut off mid-text in a booklet count
toward totals and first-author credit, but not senior-author credit or the text analyses.</li>
<li><strong>Institutions.</strong> A hand-curated list of {{ n_institutes }} institutions and their spelling variants.
Schools fold into their university (except Harvard Medical School); NIH institutes fold into NIH; hospitals and
independent institutes stay separate.</li>
<li><strong>Countries and regions.</strong> Each author's country comes from the location at the end of their
affiliations (“Seoul, Korea, Republic of”; “Boston, MA”), which nearly every affiliation in {{ years[0].year }}–2024 has. When
there is none (most of 2025–2026), it comes from the resolved institution, else from country, demonym or city names in the
affiliation (“Seoul National University”), else from a hand-checked list of the remaining affiliation strings
(mostly companies and smaller institutes; LLM-labeled, then spot-checked). Countries are grouped into UN M49 regions, with the Americas split into
Northern America and Latin America and the Caribbean. An author with affiliations in several countries counts in full
for each, so an abstract can count for several countries (once per region), and rows can add up to more than the total.
Because the first source that finds a country is used, an affiliation without its own location can be missed when
another one has it; nearly every 2022–2024 affiliation gives a location, so this is rare there. Every 2025 first- and
senior-author affiliation was reviewed (LLM pass, each correction checked by hand), and those hand-checked countries
take precedence. The 2026 affiliations the other rules miss were labeled the same way (two independent LLM passes,
disagreements checked) but not yet reviewed by hand. “Hong Kong, China” counts as Hong Kong only.</li>
<li><strong>Metro areas.</strong> Each institution is mapped by hand to a metro (US Census metropolitan areas, with
San Francisco + San Jose combined as the SF Bay Area and Durham–Chapel Hill + Raleigh as the Research Triangle).
Institutions with sites in several metros are left out.</li>
<li><strong>Center of mass.</strong> Each abstract sits at its first author's metro area (coordinates of the main
city; an abstract with first authors in k metros counts 1/k in each). The center is the geographic midpoint: the mean
of those points as 3D unit vectors, projected back to the surface. North America counts US and Canadian metros only
(about four in five US and Canadian abstracts have one). Worldwide, an abstract with a country but no metro sits at the
center of that country's placed abstracts that year, so every country keeps its full weight. North America is the UN
region (US, Canada, Bermuda; Mexico counts as Latin America). Removing host-city first authors drops abstracts with
any first author in the host metro; local groups at institutions without a metro stay in. The 95% ranges are from
2,000 bootstrap resamples of abstracts.</li>
<li><strong>Senior authors.</strong> The last listed person (consortium names skipped). Within a year, the same
person = same first + last name, and for common surnames (seen with 5+ different first names) also a shared
institution. Across years the 2022–2024 booklets give only initials, so the same person = same first initial + last
name; surnames seen with 5+ different initials need a shared institution, and two different spelled-out first names
(2025–2026) are never merged unless one is a short form of the other (Alex, Alexander).</li>
<li><strong>Phrases.</strong> 1–3-word n-grams from the abstract text, appearing in at least 5 talks (or the
equivalent share of posters). Each gets a talk-vs-poster odds ratio with +0.5 smoothing and a 95% CI, ranked by
z-score. Phrases that share a word or are an acronym of each other and appear in mostly the same abstracts (Jaccard
≥ 0.45; exact acronyms like “QTLs” always) form one group, shown under its best-ranked member. A narrower phrase
stays separate from a broader one that contains it when the broader one carries its own signal: every abstract
mentioning “chromatin accessibility” also mentions “chromatin” (Jaccard 0.43), but most “chromatin” abstracts are
about something else, and those still lean toward talks ({{ years[0].year }}–{{ years[-1].year }} talk rate 15.7%
for “chromatin” without accessibility, 21.9% with “chromatin accessibility”, 9.4% without “chromatin”).</li>
<li><strong>Phrase score.</strong> The phrases overrepresented in talks with a pooled z-score past a Bonferroni
threshold (two-sided 5% across every phrase tested), grouped as above; a text's score is the number of groups it
uses. The <a href="phrases.html#score">scorer</a> runs in the browser and matches the pipeline's scores exactly. Its
out-of-sample check rebuilds the list from the earlier years and scores the latest year (AUC: the chance a random
talk outscores a random poster, ties half).</li>
<li><strong>Words.</strong> Tokens from the title and text of each complete abstract, counted once per abstract;
English stop words, numbers and one-letter tokens are left out, and plurals are separate words.</li>
<li><strong>Genes.</strong> Approved HGNC protein-coding symbols in the title or text, case-sensitive, as whole
tokens (each part of a hyphenated token counts, but not a symbol right before “+”, as in “CD4+ T cells”). Symbols that
in abstracts mostly mean something else were checked against their contexts and left out ({{ n_ambiguous }} of them, from MAF and HR
to CD4). Positions are each gene's leftmost RefSeq transcript start on hg38 (UCSC refGene); the few genes without
one are left out of the figure.</li>
<li><strong>All years.</strong> Institution, place and author talk rates pool the counts from every year. Phrase
odds ratios across years are Mantel–Haenszel estimates stratified by year (each year's 2×2 table, +0.5 in every
cell, Robins–Breslow–Greenland variance), because the baseline talk rate differs by year.</li>
<li><strong>Intervals.</strong> Talk rates use 95% Wilson intervals.</li>
</ul>
